{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '1,?[0-9]{3}|November 6|2026-11-06|assignments|LMM' '/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF' '/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md' | head -100\"","aggregated_output":"/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:9:## Rafa to research: issuer AUM sources for the gaps (2026-11-06)\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:19:## Dropped from the etfbench pilot, for Rafa's review (2026-11-06)\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:21:These are your original etfbench questions (`questions.md`). They were left out of the 2026-11-06 pilot because neither the bank nor the drafts hold a defensible answer. For each one, decide: research it into the bank (and say how), rewrite it, or drop it from etfbench.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:32:### Candidate: Who are the 5 largest ETF lead market makers? (research 2026-11-06; Q3 target 2026-09-30)\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:34:**Awaiting Rafa's approval. Historical quarter-end ranking unresolved.** The official files available today are current snapshots, not archived September 30 assignments. The table ranks **assigned ETP securities across the retrieved US program lists**, not a verified ETF-only universe or trading-market share. No historical rank is inferred.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:36:| Rank by assignments | Exchange-normalized firm | NYSE Arca file | Nasdaq DLP | Cboe BZX LMM | Total assignments |\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:38:| 1 | Jane Street | 624 | 245 | 254 | 1,123 |\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:44:**Official sources / dates:** [NYSE ETF resources](https://www.nyse.com/products/exchange-traded-products/exchange-traded-funds) links the [current Arca workbook](https://www.nyse.com/publicdocs/nyse/markets/nyse-arca/NYSE_Arca_Equities_LMM_Current.xlsx); [Nasdaq ETF definitions](https://www.nasdaqtrader.com/trader.aspx?id=etf_definitions); [Cboe LMM securities](https://www.cboe.com/us/equities/listings/trade_on_cboe/lmm/securities/). All retrieved **2026-11-06**. NYSE has one sheet, `LMM Details 2026-10-05`, and workbook modified metadata `2026-10-05T18:37:49Z`; use **October 5 sheet date**, not September 30. No internal effective date was established for Nasdaq or Cboe. Retrieval is the observation date, not proof every record changed that day. The NYSE resources page calls it “NYSE Listed ETPs”; the workbook filename is Arca. Coverage of NYSE proper's DMM assignments has **not** been established; this is not an all-NYSE-venues ranking.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:46:**Method:** parse the NYSE XLSX with Python zipfile/XML and the other two HTML tables; deduplicate symbol/firm within each venue. NYSE: 2,742 rows with symbols/assignment text → 2,728 unique assignments excluding 13 “No LMM” rows and one duplicate symbol/firm. Nasdaq: 1,279 unique DLP assignments; exclude Market Quality Supporters. Cboe: 1,674 unique symbol/firm rows including one test account → 1,673 after removing that account. Short exchange labels are grouped by named firm (Jane Street, Virtu, GTS, Susquehanna, Citadel), not by a claimed legal-entity identifier. Totals count exchange assignments, not globally deduplicated funds; listing-transfer overlaps have not been reconciled. An ETF-only classification join remains necessary. **AUM ranking not computed:** no cheap, reliable identifier/date-matched quarter-end join was obtained. Assets supported by an LMM would measure client-fund size, not the firm's own AUM or quoting volume.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:48:**Non-US equivalents:** Xetra publishes [instrument-level designated sponsors](https://www.cashmarket.deutsche-boerse.com/cash-de/Handel/Xetra/liquiditaet-durch-designated-sponsors/liste-der-designated-sponsors/1113274%21tradable); Euronext/ETFplus publishes a [market-maker list and assignment workbook](https://www.borsaitaliana.it/etf/marketmakers/marketmakers.en.htm). LSE has a [public ETP trading framework](https://www.londonstockexchange.com/en/markets-products/asset-classes/exchange-traded-products/etps-trading), but a complete downloadable fund/market-maker mapping was not verified. ASX publishes its [ETP market-making scheme](https://www.asx.com.au/markets/market-resources/market-maker-arrangements); comprehensive public assignments were not established, although sponsor product pages sometimes name makers. [SIX](https://www.six-group.com/en/market-data/etf/market-makers.html) publishes firm/product counts. These programs can have several makers per instrument; their counts are not equivalent to one US lead assignment.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:50:**Snapshot audit:** raw files and parsed rows retained locally in `~/.local/state/fleet/etf-quarterly-rankings/initial-2026-11-06/` (not synced). SHA256: `etf-nyse.xlsx`: `970ce815e42643701f56492981950ad86cd1256cfbb871d62ded0f23ae9f6b91`; `etf-nasdaq.html`: `0acf4d3dfa85e896d2dcc5a022f6f82cff3462c66bb4b1370a223dab68969de3`; `etf-cboe.html`: `f635976a6088e0d79b03cca1eb60465921d6a43261b6f3e9408fe6d6e5901255`; `etf-lmm-rows.json`: `584b080822ec55ed251905990a2dfbad47d77ef09c0f814fadb4d4530b2dbf2b`.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:52:**Recommended etfbench wording:** “Which firms have the most US exchange LMM/DLP assignments in the dated snapshot?” Keep September 30 scoring disabled until an archived dataset exists.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:54:### Candidate: Who are the top 3 largest issuers of ETFs? (2026-09-30; researched 2026-11-06)\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:76:**Broader Europe proxy only:** [Lipper's European review](https://lipperalpha.refinitiv.com/reports/2026/10/european-etf-industry-review-year-to-date-september-2026/), September 30, October 14 report, gives iShares **EUR1,309.1bn**, Amundi **EUR420.2bn**, Xtrackers **EUR336.4bn**. Its published iShares share is **40.73%**; matching shares for the other two were not independently established from an unambiguous common denominator. This includes ETFs registered for sale in Europe and other domiciles; it is **not** the requested IE+LU UCITS ranking. Lipper's separate [global review](https://lipperalpha.refinitiv.com/reports/2026/10/global-etf-industry-review-september-2026/) gives domicile totals Ireland **USD2,716.2bn**, Luxembourg **USD668.9bn**, but no sponsor split. No allocation of a sponsor's entire European AUM to one domicile is justified. Official CBI/CSSF/EFAMA totals and registers need a sponsor-level assets join; no usable public quarter-end joined file was established.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:84:| 3 | iShares | 66.1032 | 17.57% |\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:97:| 4 | Fidelity | 69.1198 | 6.46% |\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:100:Source: [BMO ETF Industry Flows October table](https://www.bmoetfs.ca/flows), retrieved November 6; publication date absent. **Derived September 30, not a direct historical table:** October AUM minus October **total change**, not capital flows. Example RBC: CAD297,145.4m − CAD2,678.0m. Repeating for all 51 provider rows yields September denominator **CAD1,070,189.5m**. October source HTML retained in the local initial snapshot directory as `etf-bmo-october.html` (SHA256 `263fa3959397f4dd710cf49da92c0bc766c8854ac9e1468b5b4f1f1ae8d7c48b`). Conditional on unchanged provider grouping and restatement policy; mergers/reclassifications could impair reconstruction. All listed ETF AUM may include holdings in other ETFs. RBC iShares is a commercial alliance, not a single legal manager. [SIMA](https://sima-amvi.ca/research-statistics/statistics/) removes Canadian ETF-in-ETF double counting; its total is therefore not this denominator. CETFA's September “ETF Insights” PDF was inaccessible and appears to concern investor research; its title alone does not establish a sponsor league. Seek a direct September industry table next refresh.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:112:Source: [Japan FinTech Observer, Q3 Morningstar report coverage](https://www.fintechobserver.com/japan-etf-assets-hit-record-jpy-137-8trn-in-q3-as-equity-inflows-rebound-and-bond-offerings-surge/), end September; article retrieved November 6, exact publication date not established. Domestic ETF universe **JPY137.8trn**. Secondary reporting of commercial research, not JPX's own sponsor table. Reported shares are rounded and not exactly reproducible from rounded balances (Daiwa, for example); retained as reported. Nomura's [November SEC filing](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1163653/000119312526442521/d522871d6k.htm) separately shows September ETF market JPY136.2trn and rounded Nomura share 44%: a useful primary cross-check with a different population, not a replacement denominator.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:122:The [September summary](https://unjena.tistory.com/1739?category=1095859) is a blog, not sufficient for etfbench truth. These are leads for verification, not approved facts. [KRX ETF statistics](https://data.krx.co.kr/contents/MDC/EASY/main/MDCEASY008.jsp) exposes date filters but no filled historical table in the fetched page. Need September 30 net assets by management company, ETF-only excluding ETNs; do not derive historical AUM from today's KRX-based pages or Samsung's year-end 2025 profile book.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:134:Source: [Shanghai Securities News via Eastmoney, November 2](https://finance.eastmoney.com/a/202611023918889389.html), **September 30 column**, Tianxiang data; converted from CNY100m to billions. This is neither an official exchange league nor the October 29 ranking (where positions 3–4 switch). [October 8 Cailian/Wind report](https://cj.sina.com.cn/articles/view/2868676035/aafc85c302001ycrs) reports September total **CNY4.92trn**, E Fund **CNY627.671bn**, ChinaAMC **CNY620.164bn**. Vendors disagree, particularly on E Fund; no market shares are computed by mixing Tianxiang numerators with Wind's total. Resolve coverage of money-market, commodity, bond and QDII ETFs, valuation lags and feeder exclusions against sponsor/exchange quarter-end files before scoring.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:146:**Method and source quality:** official exchange assignments establish present LMM roles, not past roles or ETF-only coverage. Official ASX is strong within its ETP scope. US and European Lipper are commercial datasets, not official registries. Canada is a labelled calculation from issuer research, pending a direct historical table. Japan is secondary Morningstar reporting with a primary Nomura cross-check. China is conflicting commercial-vendor reporting. Korea is an unverified research lead. HKEX supplies a strong total but no sponsor league. IE, LU and IE+LU UCITS remain unresolved. No missing ranking is filled from memory.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:151:2. **LMM measure:** default count exchange assignments and label ETP coverage; add ETF-only and assigned-fund AUM only with an auditable dated join. A September archive is needed for the original Q3 question.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:154:**Knowledge processed:** exchange LMM/DLP assignment mechanics; sponsor/brand versus legal issuer; quarter-end regional assets and source populations; domicile versus listing; ETF/ETP and ETF-share-class counting; publication lags and data gaps.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:163:- **Transfer agent:** issues the AP's PINs, receives creation and redemption orders and sends the NSCC instructions. ESS form (2012): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001540305/000089418912002911/ap_agmt.htm · ActiveShares (2020): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1792795/000119312520015437/d822612dex99e2.htm · high\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:168:  - Rule 6c-11 adds no extra conditions for self-indexed ETFs. The SEC found portfolio transparency plus existing law (Rules 38a-1 and 17j-1, Advisers Act policies) enough, which dropped the firewall conditions of earlier orders. https://www.sec.gov/files/rules/final/2019/33-10695.pdf (pp. 23–25) · high\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:178:- **The fund, UCITS liquidity tools (Directive 2024/927):** a UCITS must choose at least two liquidity management tools from the Directive's list and notify its regulator when it activates or deactivates one. ESMA wrote technical standards and guidelines on them. https://www.mondaq.com/ireland/fund-management-reits/1753970/central-bank-updates-on-aifmd-iiucits-vi-processes-and-eu-publication-of-final-lmt-rts · medium\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:188:- **Distributor:** in iShares (2018 SAI), keeps records of orders and acceptances and holds the AP agreements. https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001524513/000119312518108948/d561568d485bpos.htm · high\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:190:- **Securities-lending agent:** can be the adviser's affiliate under SEC relief. iShares (2018): BlackRock Institutional Trust Co.; the fund keeps 80% of lending income (85% after a hurdle), never less than 70% of income plus collateral-investment fees, with those fees capped at 0.04% a year. https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001524513/000119312518108948/d561568d485bpos.htm · high\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:192:- **AP:** must be in NSCC's clearing process or a DTC participant. May post US-dollar cash collateral of at least 105% for deposit securities not yet delivered, and keeps the income on it. https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001524513/000119312518108948/d561568d485bpos.htm; collateral from search excerpts · medium\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:196:- **Adviser:** advisers generally don't recover NAV-error overpayments from redeeming shareholders (fund policy). https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001329954/000113322815001315/e405429_ex99-h9.htm · high\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:205:    - The portfolio belongs to the fund, separately from its adviser; adviser creditors do not acquire a claim on that portfolio just because the adviser fails. Sponsor failure therefore does not by itself wipe out ETF shares. [ICI presentation to the Federal Reserve, “Protecting Fund Assets — Custody”](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/rr-commpublic/ici_meeting_20110104.pdf#page=1).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:213:    - In one filed 2026 liquidation, shareholders receive NAV after charges, taxes, expenses and liabilities, with possible final income/gain distributions; taxable holders can realize a gain or loss. [One + One ETF liquidation supplement, March 13, 2026](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1884021/000121390026027879/ea0281711-02_497.htm). General tax principle: liquidation distributions recover basis, with excess taxable as capital gain; a loss may await the final distribution. [IRS Publication 550, “Liquidating Distributions”](https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:230:  - Where asked: [Bogleheads forum](https://www.bogleheads.org/forum/viewtopic.php?t=322565); [Bogleheads forum](https://www.bogleheads.org/forum/viewtopic.php?p=612979).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:235:  - Where asked: [Bogleheads forum](https://www.bogleheads.org/forum/viewtopic.php?t=322565); [Bogleheads forum](https://www.bogleheads.org/forum/viewtopic.php?p=612979).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:248:  - Where asked: [ICI ETF Basics and Structure FAQ](https://www.ici.org/faqs/faqs_etfs); [Money Stack Exchange, Understanding how an ETF works](https://money.stackexchange.com/questions/126592/understanding-how-an-etf-works).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:253:  - Where asked: [Money Stack Exchange, Understanding how an ETF works](https://money.stackexchange.com/questions/126592/understanding-how-an-etf-works); [ICI ETF Basics and Structure FAQ](https://www.ici.org/faqs/faqs_etfs).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:258:  - Where asked: [ICI ETF Basics and Structure FAQ](https://www.ici.org/faqs/faqs_etfs); [Money Stack Exchange, Understanding how an ETF works](https://money.stackexchange.com/questions/126592/understanding-how-an-etf-works).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:290:  - Already covered: [[ETF Participants#Market makers and the lead market maker (LMM)]].\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:302:    - Cost allocation is contractual: in Lazard's filed response, the manager bears the new ETF's organization costs without recoupment, and pays trustees directly under its unitary fee. [Lazard SEC correspondence, comments 11–12, 2025](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2051630/000093041325001085/filename1.htm).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:310:    - A filed example elects the 75-day route and states that shares cannot be sold before effectiveness. [Simplify registration amendment, January 30, 2024, cover](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1810747/000182912624000561/simplifyetf_485apos.htm).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:332:    - A distributor appointment is not a promise to gather assets. In Simplify's filing, Foreside is principal underwriter/national distributor and receives its annual fee and expenses from the adviser's resources. [Simplify SAI, “Distributor”](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1810747/000182912624000561/simplifyetf_485apos.htm).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:377:  - **Research draft (2026-11-05; awaiting Rafa approval):** **Target:** [[ETF Participants#Board]] · **Facet: Duties — LMM oversight** · US ('40 Act), ETF.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:378:    - Ask why the exchange/LMM was chosen, what the LMM knows about the relevant market, and what capital—including seed capital—it is willing to commit. [IDC oversight paper, “ETF Contractual Relationships”](https://www.idc.org/idc/issues/types/etf/publications/ci.idc_12_etfs).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:379:    - **Confidence: high** as suggested board inquiry, not a prescribed approval checklist or a claim that every LMM provides seed capital.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:393:    - **Proposed diagnosis (medium; synthesis):** test stale NAV inputs/market-hour differences, underlying trading costs and impediments to creation/redemption before assuming an arbitrage failure. The SEC discusses these frictions in its adopting release. [Release 33-10695, sections I.B and II.C](https://www.sec.gov/files/rules/final/2019/33-10695.pdf).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:401:    - A filed prospectus also identifies cash holdings and dividend-accrual timing as sources of divergence. [Simplify prospectus, “Tracking Error Risk”](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1810747/000182912624000561/simplifyetf_485apos.htm).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:414:  - Recurrence: Regulator Q&A ID 1012, revised in 2025; recurrence indicated by revision, not a measured count.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:417:    - CBI permits daily holdings on a nondiscriminatory basis, or periodic holdings with daily arbitrage information available nondiscriminatorily to APs/market makers, prospectus disclosure of that information, procedures for impaired arbitrage and investor information requests, and public quarter-end holdings within 30 business days. [CBI Q&A 1012, pp. 5–6, April 2025](https://www.centralbank.ie/docs/default-source/regulation/industry-market-sectors/funds/ucits/guidance/42nd-edition-ucits-qa.pdf#page=5).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:422:  - Recurrence: Regulator Q&A ID 1016; frequency not measured.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:424:    - Use “UCITS ETF” in the name, constitution, prospectus, KID/KIID and marketing. For a mixed umbrella, identify the ETF sub-fund; for mixed listed/unlisted classes, the identifier may be at sub-fund or listed-class level, with the classes clearly distinguished. [2026 regulations, Reg. 88(1)–(2)](https://www.centralbank.ie/docs/default-source/regulation/industry-market-sectors/funds/ucits/s-i-316-of-2026---central-bank-%28supervision-and-enforcement%29-act-2013-%28section-48%281%29%29-%28undertakings-for-collective-investment-in-transferable-securities%29-regul.pdf#page=64); [CBI Q&A 1016 and 1088](https://www.centralbank.ie/docs/default-source/regulation/industry-market-sectors/funds/ucits/guidance/42nd-edition-ucits-qa.pdf#page=6).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:429:  - Recurrence: Regulator Q&A ID 1017; frequency not measured.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:434:  - Recurrence: Regulator Q&A ID 1088; frequency not measured.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:436:    - Listed and unlisted classes may coexist. The prospectus comparison table covers dealing access/frequency, settlement, broker use, costs, minimum amounts and shareholder rights; it also addresses unlisted-class liquidity and differences in class prices. [CBI Q&A 1088, pp. 6–7](https://www.centralbank.ie/docs/default-source/regulation/industry-market-sectors/funds/ucits/guidance/42nd-edition-ucits-qa.pdf#page=6).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:441:  - Recurrence: Regulator Q&A ID 1030; frequency not measured.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/00 Inbox/ETF inbox.md:444:    - **Historical distinction:** Q&A 1030 required a reasoned waiver application; do not carry that old requirement into the express 2026 exceptions. [CBI Q&A 1030, April 2025](https://www.centralbank.ie/docs/default-source/regulation/industry-market-sectors/funds/ucits/guidance/42nd-edition-ucits-qa.pdf#page=14).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Structure and Regulation.md:3:The wrappers and legal forms an ETF can take, and the rules that come with each. Part of the [[ETF]] knowledge bank; this note is framework layer 2. It starts with the legal forms of the umbrella vehicle; the rest of the layer (1940 Act ETFs, Rule 6c-11, other wrappers) gets written here as it's reviewed.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Market Mechanics.md:47:- **Transaction fees — US ('40 Act), ETF:** “I believe” the typical maximum variable creation/redemption fee is 3% (Rafael, 2026-11-06 (practitioner knowledge; may not be public)). Corgi's filed SAI specifies “up to 3%”; that is a filing example, not a universal cap. [Corgi ETF Trust I SAI, “Creation Transaction Fees” / “Redemption Transaction Fees”](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2078265/000182912625010217/corgietftrusti_n-1aa.htm).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Market Mechanics.md:51:- **Basket settlement — US ('40 Act), ETF:** stocks and bonds can be delivered in kind. Derivatives in creations and redemptions are cash, though people are developing workarounds. One anecdotal example: when the AP and the swap counterparty are the same broker, a swap may be “redeemed in kind” (Rafael, 2026-11-06 (practitioner knowledge; may not be public); anecdotal). That example does not establish that derivatives are generally transferable in kind. A fund using cash settlement loses much of the in-kind benefit.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Market Mechanics.md:55:- Dave Mazza, thread on ETF tax efficiency and custom baskets (2023-05-22): https://x.com/MazzaDave/status/1660793487447715841\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Market Mechanics.md:57:- Rafael, 2026-11-06 (practitioner knowledge; may not be public): cash settlement of derivatives and the anecdotal swap workaround; “I believe” a typical maximum variable fee of 3%.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Market Mechanics.md:58:- Corgi ETF Trust I, filed SAI (2025-12-22), variable creation/redemption fees up to 3%, fixed fees to the custodian and variable fees to the fund: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2078265/000182912625010217/corgietftrusti_n-1aa.htm\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Market Mechanics.md:60:- Rafael (practitioner knowledge, 2026-11-06; approved by Rafa): creation/redemption fee wording and resolution of the fee conflict.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Market Mechanics.md:61:- SEC Rule 6c-11 adopting release, transaction fees covering processing and brokerage costs to prevent dilution (p. 12 n. 27; pp. 57–58): https://www.sec.gov/files/rules/final/2019/33-10695.pdf\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Market Mechanics.md:62:- Putnam ETF Trust prospectus (2025), creation/redemption fees offsetting transfer and transaction costs: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1845809/000119312525187238/d842776d485bpos.htm\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Market Mechanics.md:63:- Davis SAI supplement (2024-07-01), fixed processing fees and variable cash fees: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1670310/000007170124000048/497.htm\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Tax Efficiency.md:7:ETFs and mutual funds are usually both 1940 Act funds taxed as regulated investment companies (IRS RICs). An IRS RIC passes its net realized capital gains through to shareholders as taxable distributions every year. So the question is how much a fund *realizes*, not whether realized gains reach shareholders.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Tax Efficiency.md:17:The fund's nonrecognition rule does not exempt the AP. On a redemption treated as an exchange, the AP generally recognizes the difference between the redemption value and its basis in the ETF shares. Recently purchased shares may produce little gain. The securities received take a cost basis generally equal to the value of the shares surrendered, rather than inheriting the fund's low basis. A later sale measures gain against that new basis. [IRS Notice 2026-62, §2.01](https://www.irs.gov/irb/2026-43_irb); [IRC §1001](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1001).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Tax Efficiency.md:33:UCITS: there are no heartbeat trades; the rules differ from the US (Rafael, 2026-11-06 (practitioner knowledge; may not be public)).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Tax Efficiency.md:49:The US fund-level mechanism does not determine every investor's tax treatment. For a UK investor in an offshore reporting fund, reportable income includes undistributed excess income; gains on disposal generally receive capital-gains treatment if reporting status held throughout ownership. Non-reporting fund gains are generally offshore income gains instead. [HMRC overview](https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/investment-funds/ifm12446).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Tax Efficiency.md:57:- Dave Mazza, thread on ETF tax efficiency and custom baskets (2023-05-22), including the heartbeat chart: https://x.com/MazzaDave/status/1660793487447715841\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Tax Efficiency.md:59:- Rafael, 2026-11-06 (practitioner knowledge; may not be public): different UCITS rules and no UCITS heartbeat trades.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/Advisory Contract Approval.md:49:- ESMA supervisory briefing on costs (2020): https://www.esma.europa.eu/sites/default/files/library/esma34-39-1042_supervisory_briefing_on_the_supervision_of_costs.pdf\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Share Class Board Considerations.md:5:**Source scope (checked 2026-11-05):** the three sections on adviser reports, monitoring thresholds and class allocation below distinguish binding undertakings from suggested diligence. The other sections remain working diligence topics, not an assertion that every bullet is a condition of relief. Read the particular fund’s order and incorporated application; an application alone is not permission to launch. The [DFA order](https://www.sec.gov/files/rules/ic/2025/ic-35786.pdf) grants relief subject to its application’s representations and conditions. The saved [Tweedy Browne application](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/732905/000119312525228681/d79289d40appa.htm) supplies the detailed template used below; its [January 13, 2026 order IC-35896](https://www.sec.gov/files/rules/ic/2026/ic-35896.pdf) is the corresponding authorization.\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Share Class Board Considerations.md:50:This is **transition analysis**, not a requirement to sell every appreciated security or distribute all embedded gains when the ETF class is added. Actual realization depends on the portfolio changes and transactions undertaken. [Tweedy Browne application, §IV(a), Initial Evaluation and Approval, pp. 3–4 and associated footnotes](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/732905/000119312525228681/d79289d40appa.htm).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Share Class Board Considerations.md:64:The board must consider whether the findings require additional action and renew the best-interest determination for each class and the whole fund through a majority of directors and a majority of independent directors. [Tweedy Browne application, §IV(c), Ongoing Board Approval; §VII, conditions 5–6](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/732905/000119312525228681/d79289d40appa.htm).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Share Class Board Considerations.md:72:**Breach procedure:** notify the board **no later than 30 days after the end of the applicable measurement period in which the threshold was exceeded**, rather than 30 days after the trade or discovery. Notification may go to a designated board committee or member. Provide a written assessment of the causes and proposed recommendations for what remediation, if any, the fund should take. A breach triggers review; it is not an automatic finding of unlawful subsidization or a mandatory closure. [Tweedy Browne application, §IV(b), Ongoing Monitoring Process, pp. 4–5 and footnotes](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/732905/000119312525228681/d79289d40appa.htm).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Share Class Board Considerations.md:84:The application gives **examples**, not mandatory responses: change in-kind use or execution; use credit lines or other cash sources; improve tax-lot management/loss harvesting; adjust lawful transaction, purchase or redemption fees; enhance disclosure; discontinue or convert a class as otherwise permitted. The appropriate response depends on the facts; borrowing and fees introduce their own costs. [Tweedy Browne application, §IV(b), remedial-action footnote](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/732905/000119312525228681/d79289d40appa.htm).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Share Class Board Considerations.md:86:Approval/report documents and records showing thresholds **exceeded or not exceeded**, plus monitoring materials provided to the board, must be kept at least six years, first two easily accessible. [Same application, §VII, condition 8](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/732905/000119312525228681/d79289d40appa.htm).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Share Class Board Considerations.md:130:Each class’s per-share NAV is its attributed assets minus liabilities, divided by its shares outstanding. The same underlying portfolio need not produce the same dollar NAV per share: class share counts, initial per-share values, accumulated expense differences and distribution policies can differ. ETF **market price** is a separate number, determined by exchange trading; a premium/discount or intraday price move does not itself explain an interclass NAV allocation difference. [Rule 6c-11 definitions](https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/17/270.6c-11); [Tweedy Browne application, §VI, ETF Class Relief](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/732905/000119312525228681/d79289d40appa.htm).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Share Class Board Considerations.md:138:For funds other than the daily/monthly case below, the application expects mutual fund dividends to be declared on the ex-dividend date and ETF dividends one business day earlier, with **all classes’ NAVs adjusted on the same ex-dividend date**. Different announcement dates alone therefore do not create an economic benefit. Under the described T+1 conventions, mutual fund record date is the prior business day and ETF record date is ex-date; purchases in either class on the prior day still receive the dividend. These are the application’s contemplated arrangements, not a universal calendar for every fund. [Tweedy Browne application, §VI, dividend declaration/record-date discussion and footnotes 32–36](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/732905/000119312525228681/d79289d40appa.htm).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Share Class Board Considerations.md:148:Apply these adjusted weights to **income, realized gains/losses and unrealized appreciation/depreciation** (the application’s “Allocable Items”). This changes the allocation base; it does **not** force class NAVs to match, retroactively declare a dividend or transfer an investor’s cash dividend between classes. The board must determine that the adjusted method generally leaves annualized class returns differing only by expense differentials, as required for the fair-method alternative under Rule 18f-3(c)(1)(v). [Tweedy Browne application, §VI, Asset Adjustment and footnotes 33–35](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/732905/000119312525228681/d79289d40appa.htm).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Share Class Board Considerations.md:156:Mutual fund investors may reinvest on ex-date; ETF investors generally wait for payment several days later, with automatic reinvestment dependent on their broker. The dividend amount can be out of market during that interval, helping or hurting ETF reinvestment performance as prices move. The Asset Adjustment addresses declaration-based allocation distortion; it does not eliminate payment/reinvestment timing effects. These effects belong in the ongoing report. [Tweedy Browne application, §VI, dividend payment/reinvestment discussion, pp. 14–15](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/732905/000119312525228681/d79289d40appa.htm).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Share Class Board Considerations.md:232:- SEC DFA amended application, September 26, 2025: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/355437/000207184425000084/dfa40appamend092025.htm\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF.md:40:   - [[ETF Participants#The trust (umbrella vehicle)|The trust (umbrella vehicle)]] · [[ETF Participants#The fund|The fund]] · [[ETF Participants#Sponsor|Sponsor]] · [[ETF Participants#Management company (UCITS)|Management company (UCITS)]] · [[ETF Participants#Adviser and sub-adviser|Adviser and sub-adviser]] · [[ETF Participants#Board|Board]] · [[ETF Participants#Custodian|Custodian]] · [[ETF Participants#Fund accountant / administrator|Fund accountant / administrator]] · [[ETF Participants#Transfer agent|Transfer agent]] · [[ETF Participants#Distributor|Distributor]] · [[ETF Participants#Index provider|Index provider]] · [[ETF Participants#Authorized participant (AP)|Authorized participant (AP)]] · [[ETF Participants#Market makers and the lead market maker (LMM)|Market makers and the lead market maker (LMM)]] · [[ETF Participants#Exchange / listing venue|Exchange / listing venue]] · [[ETF Participants#DTCC (NSCC and DTC)|DTCC (NSCC and DTC)]] · [[ETF Participants#Other service providers|Other service providers]] · [[ETF Participants#ETF investor|ETF investor]]\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF.md:43:   - **1940 Act open-end ETF** (the deep subtopic): '40-Act RIC (registered investment company) · IRS RIC status (Subchapter M) · Rule 6c-11 (including the written-policy requirement for custom baskets) · the exemptive-relief era before 6c-11 · index vs active · transparent vs semi-transparent\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF.md:55:   - Launch: seeding, listing, LMM arrangements\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Share Class Structure.md:9:Rule 6c-11 supplies ordinary ETF operational exemptions but deliberately excluded this share-class structure. Different redemption, trading and dividend rights also go beyond the ordinary multiple-class rule. Orders provide ETF operational relief and relief from the one-class/equal-rights constraints (Investment Company Act sections 18(f)(1) and 18(i)); simply following Rule 6c-11 and Rule 18f-3 is insufficient. [SEC 2019 adopting release, pp. 122–124](https://www.sec.gov/files/rules/final/2019/33-10695.pdf).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Share Class Structure.md:11:**Status checked 2026-11-05:** relief extends beyond DFA; for example, [Tortoise’s order IC-36181](https://www.sec.gov/files/rules/ic/2026/ic-36181.pdf) was issued May 27, 2026. The SEC counted approximately **100 applications as of March 17, 2026**, so the raw research’s “75 managers” is stale and counts a different unit. That March order also supplies conditional broker-dealer relief for specified ETF-class transactions; it does not cover the mutual fund classes or the exchange privilege. [SEC Exchange Act order 34-105028, pp. 1–3](https://www.sec.gov/files/rules/exorders/2026/34-105028.pdf).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Share Class Structure.md:13:Applications continued after June: [Wasatch’s September 2026 notice](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-09-21/pdf/2026-19212.pdf) is a notice, not an approval. The September 30 proposal to expand multiple classes concerns **closed-end funds and BDCs**, not replacement of the open-end ETF-class orders. No superseding open-end ETF-class rule was identified in this check; this is not a complete count of current orders. [SEC proposal IC-36351](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-10-05/html/2026-20360.htm).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Share Class Structure.md:47:[Primary application: Tweedy Browne, §VII, conditions 1–9](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/732905/000119312525228681/d79289d40appa.htm), also preserved in the raw application collection. The reports, thresholds and allocation mechanics are developed in [[ETF Share Class Board Considerations]].\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Share Class Structure.md:49:Disclosure commitments include identifying both class types in prospectuses/websites, explaining ETF trading and individual-redemption limits and any exchange privilege, plain-language website education, and warning that one class’s transactions may create costs and taxes for the other. [Same application, §VI, Disclosure Steps](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/732905/000119312525228681/d79289d40appa.htm).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Share Class Structure.md:53:An offered exchange privilege generally runs **mutual fund → ETF**, at relative NAV, rather than at ETF market price; it is not an ordinary right to exchange back. Dividend-related restrictions and the actual fund’s terms matter. [Tweedy Browne application, Exchange Privilege](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/732905/000119312525228681/d79289d40appa.htm).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Participants.md:192:  - US ('40 Act), ETF: launch economics depend on the trust approach. Using an existing, mature multi-series trust costs from about $150,000 a year upward (Rafael, 2026-11-06 (practitioner knowledge; may not be public)).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Participants.md:200:    - US ('40 Act), ETF: white-label (series-trust) platforms sometimes sell marketing and distribution support as add-ons. It depends on the platform; it is not universal (Rafael, 2026-11-06 (practitioner knowledge; may not be public)).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Participants.md:538:  - UCITS (Ireland): authorised and supervised by the Central Bank as an investment firm (Investment Intermediaries Act 1995).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Participants.md:551:  - Concentration: a single accounting platform's failure can delay NAVs across a whole industry segment. The 2015 failure of one administrator's platform delayed NAVs for about 1,200 fund structures (Sources).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Participants.md:599:  - US ('40 Act), ETF: sponsors pay APs only indirectly, through the funds' commissions (Rafael, 2026-11-06 (practitioner knowledge; may not be public)).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Participants.md:600:  - UCITS: the rules differ from the US, and there are no heartbeat trades (Rafael, 2026-11-06 (practitioner knowledge; may not be public); see [[ETF Tax Efficiency#Custom rebalances and heartbeat trades]]).\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Participants.md:602:## Market makers and the lead market maker (LMM)\n/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Participants.md:606:**Economics:** the market maker earns the bid-ask spread on the exchange and is often the same firm as an AP. It pays its hedging and creation/redemption costs out of the spread. Exchanges may pay LMMs incentives for quoting thinly traded ETFs.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
